WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.727 --> 00:00:10.640
A vaccine is a substance that&nbsp;
teaches your body to recognize and fight off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:17.200
things like viruses and bacteria. Vaccines work&nbsp;
by looking as much like the virus or bacteria as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:22.160
possible without causing the harmful effects of&nbsp;
the disease. Vaccines can be made from a weakened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:28.240
form of the virus itself; from unique pieces of&nbsp;
the virus; from a chemical that the virus creates;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.160
or even from substances that scientists&nbsp;
make that are similar to parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.240
virus. After you receive a vaccine, your&nbsp;
immune system will recognize and attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.840
the virus or bacteria if you are exposed to&nbsp;
it later. This means that you will either not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.560
become ill at all, or you will have a milder&nbsp;
response to the infection. Vaccines use your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.600
own body to fight off infection. Vaccines are&nbsp;
very effective — and they’re the best protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.000
against many serious diseases. Most people who get&nbsp;
vaccinated will be protected against the disease.

